;; Sizes: (64-bit)
;;   net.IP:     24 byte slice header + {4, 16} = 28 to 40 bytes
;;   net.IPAddr: 40 byte slice header + {4, 16} = 44 to 56 bytes + zone length
;;   netip.Addr: 24 bytes (zone is per-name singleton, shared across all users)









(go/type Addr "Addr represents an IPv4 or IPv6 address (with or without a scoped\naddressing zone), similar to [net.IP] or [net.IPAddr].\n\nUnlike [net.IP] or [net.IPAddr], Addr is a comparable value\ntype (it supports == and can be a map key) and is immutable.\n\nThe zero Addr is not a valid IP address.\nAddr{} is distinct from both 0.0.0.0 and ::.\n" (struct















    ^{:line 53 :tag uint128 :doc "addr is the hi and lo bits of an IPv6 address. If z==z4,\nhi and lo contain the IPv4-mapped IPv6 address.\n\nhi and lo are constructed by interpreting a 16-byte IPv6\naddress as a big-endian 128-bit number. The most significant\nbits of that number go into hi, the rest into lo.\n\nFor example, 0011:2233:4455:6677:8899:aabb:ccdd:eeff is stored as:\n addr.hi = 0x0011223344556677\n addr.lo = 0x8899aabbccddeeff\n\nWe store IPs like this, rather than as [16]byte, because it\nturns most operations on IPs into arithmetic and bit-twiddling\noperations on 64-bit registers, which is much faster than\nbytewise processing.\n"} addr


    ^{:line 56 :tag (unique/Handle addrDetail) :doc "Details about the address, wrapped up together and canonicalized.\n"} z))



